+++
title = "Plugins"
template = "docs"
+++

Plugins add features to Revela. In the **Standalone** edition all official plugins are already built in, so there is nothing to install. The **Full** edition installs them as packages from the bundled `packages/` folder or from the `.nupkg` files attached to each [GitHub Release](https://github.com/Spectara/Revela/releases); they are not published on NuGet.org yet.

## What the official plugins do

<div class="features-grid">

### 📦 Compression

Pre-compress HTML, CSS, and JS with Gzip and Brotli for 70-95% smaller files.

### 📊 Statistics

Analyze EXIF data from your photos – cameras, lenses, apertures, ISO, and timeline.

### 🌐 Dev Server

Preview your generated site locally during development.

### ☁️ OneDrive Source

Sync photos from OneDrive shared folders directly into your project.

### 📅 Availability Calendar

Show booked and free days from an iCal file as a month calendar.

### 🔗 Calendar Feeds

Download iCal feeds from your booking tool into your project before each build.

</div>

## Official Plugins

| Plugin | Package | Description |
|--------|---------|-------------|
| [**Compression**](compression/) | `Spectara.Revela.Plugins.Compress` | Pre-compress static files with Gzip and Brotli |
| [**Statistics**](statistics/) | `Spectara.Revela.Plugins.Statistics` | Generate EXIF statistics pages |
| [**Serve**](serve/) | `Spectara.Revela.Plugins.Serve` | Local development server |
| [**OneDrive**](source-onedrive/) | `Spectara.Revela.Plugins.Source.OneDrive` | Download photos from OneDrive shared folders |
| [**Calendar**](calendar/) | `Spectara.Revela.Plugins.Calendar` | Availability calendar (booked/free days) from iCal `.ics` files |
| [**Source.Calendar**](source-calendar/) | `Spectara.Revela.Plugins.Source.Calendar` | Download iCal feeds (e.g. booking exports) into the source folder |

The Lumina theme has two matching extensions: **Lumina.Statistics** and **Lumina.Calendar**.

All `Spectara.Revela.*` release packages come with a `SHA256SUMS` file and Sigstore (cosign) signatures on the GitHub Release.

## Installing Plugins

How you get plugins depends on your edition:

- **Standalone**: all six official plugins and both theme extensions are built in. There is nothing to install, and the `plugin`, `packages` and `restore` commands are not available. `revela info` shows how many plugins and themes are loaded, and `revela theme list` lists the themes.
- **Full**: the `packages/` folder next to the executable is used as a local feed (`bundled`), so the commands below work offline.
- **.NET tool**: there is no bundled folder. Download the `.nupkg` files from the release into a folder and register it once with `revela config feed add releases /absolute/path/to/folder`.

The rest of this page applies to the **Full** edition and the .NET tool.

```bash
# Install by short name
revela plugin install Statistics

# Or use full package ID
revela plugin install Spectara.Revela.Plugins.Statistics

# Install a specific version
revela plugin install Statistics --version 1.2.0
```

Installing records the package and its exact installed version under `dependencies.packages` in exactly one file: `project.json` when you run it inside a project, otherwise your global `revela.json` (see [Configuration](../guide/configuration/#dependencies-and-feeds)). Uninstalling removes it from the same file. Themes and theme extensions are installed with `revela theme install`, not `plugin install`.

## Managing Plugins

```bash
# List installed plugins
revela plugin list

# Remove a plugin
revela plugin uninstall Statistics

# Refresh the package index and search it
revela packages refresh
revela packages search --type plugin
```

## Configuration

Plugins store their settings in `project.json` below `plugins`, each under its own key (`serve`, `statistics`, `oneDrive`, `calendarFeeds`). This works the same in every edition:

```json
{
  "plugins": {
    "statistics": {
      "maxEntriesPerCategory": 15,
      "sortByCount": true
    },
    "serve": {
      "port": 8080
    }
  }
}
```

Settings can also be overridden with environment variables (`SPECTARA__REVELA__PLUGINS__<KEY>__<SETTING>`):

```bash
SPECTARA__REVELA__PLUGINS__SERVE__PORT=3000
```

## Community Plugins

In the Full edition and the .NET tool, Revela supports third-party plugins from any NuGet source. Community plugins use their own package prefix:

```bash
# Install from custom NuGet feed
revela plugin install MyPlugin --source https://my-feed.example.com/v3/index.json
```

> **Note:** Community plugins are not verified by Spectara. Install at your own risk.

## Restoring Dependencies

In the Full edition and the .NET tool, use `restore` after cloning a project, updating Revela, or sharing your project with someone else, to install all required themes and plugins:

```bash
# Install missing themes and plugins
revela restore

# Check only (don't install)
revela restore --check
```

Revela reads `dependencies.packages` and `theme.name` from your configuration (global `revela.json` plus `project.json`) and installs anything that's missing. Every package in `dependencies.packages` is required, whatever its ID; installed packages are matched by package ID across plugins and themes. Restoring pins the exact installed version for the entries `project.json` declares; packages from your global `revela.json` are installed without being copied into the project.

Feeds declared only in `project.json` are never used silently. Restore lists them and asks for confirmation; in scripts and CI, where Revela cannot ask, it stops unless you pass `--allow-project-feeds`:

```bash
revela restore --allow-project-feeds
```
